This method constitutes a two-step process to find isosurface points for polynomial data. It solves the problem of finding accurate root locations for polynomial fields where standard Newton-Raphson methods fail due to poor initial guesses. It sets itself apart from pure image-order ray casting by performing root finding in object space first, using gradient integration to guide seeds to the surface, thereby ensuring the subsequent directional Newton-Raphson steps converge reliably. The method operates in two distinct stages: object-space seed projection and image-space refinement. All computations are performed independently for each seed point, requiring no neighborhood information. Inputs: A high-order multivariate polynomial function f(\mathbf{x}) defining the implicit surface, a target isovalue (typically 0), and a uniform grid of cells covering the domain. Outputs: A set of surface-tangent quads covering the isosurface in object space, which serve as rasterization targets for the next phase. Seed Point: A 3D vector \mathbf{x} in reference space. Quad: Defined by a center point (the projected seed) and a tangent plane basis, generated after projection. Seeds are distributed uniformly within each cell of the domain grid. The position \mathbf{x}0 of a seed is chosen randomly or deterministically within the cell boundaries. To mitigate the failure of Newton-Raphson when starting points are far from the solution, seeds are guided along the gradient field towards the isosurface. This integration step brings the seeds closer to the zero-crossing. Once seeds are guided close to the isosurface, Directional Newton-Raphson (NR) is applied to refine the projection onto the surface. This method is chosen over standard NR because it exhibits quadratic convergence. Formula: The iteration updates the point position \mathbf{x} as follows: \mathbf{x}{k+1} = \mathbf{x}k -  rac{f(\mathbf{x}k)}{
abla f(\mathbf{x}k) \cdot 
abla f(\mathbf{x}k)} 
abla f(\mathbf{x}k) Where: k is the iteration index (k = 0, 1, \dots). \mathbf{x}k is the point position in reference space at step k. f(\mathbf{x}k) is the value of the polynomial for the current cell at \mathbf{x}k. 
abla f(\mathbf{x}k) is the gradient of the polynomial at \mathbf{x}k. 
abla f(\mathbf{x}k) \cdot 
abla f(\mathbf{x}k) is the dot product of the gradient with itself (squared magnitude). Pitfall: Standard Newton-Raphson may fail if uniformly distributed seeds are not close to the solution. The preceding gradient integration step is critical to mitigate this. For each successfully projected point, a surface-tangent quad is generated. These quads are centered at the projected seed and aligned with the surface normal (gradient) at that point. These quads cover the isosurface and serve as the input for the image-space refinement phase. The quads generated in Step 4 are rasterized in image space. Fragments generated during rasterization serve as starting points for ray casting to further refine the isosurface representation. Process: 1. Rasterization: Rasterize the surface-tangent quads in image space to obtain fragment coordinates. 2. Transformation: Transform the ray direction and fragment coordinates into the reference coordinate system. 3. Ray-Isosurface Intersection: Perform ray-isosurface intersection computations using directional NR along the ray direction. Formula for Ray Casting: The directional NR iteration for fragment projection along a ray vector \mathbf{r} is: \mathbf{x}{k+1} = \mathbf{x}k -  rac{f(\mathbf{x}k)}{
abla f(\mathbf{x}k) \cdot \mathbf{r}} \mathbf{r} Where: \mathbf{x}k is the point position in reference space. f(\mathbf{x}k) is the polynomial value. 
abla f(\mathbf{x}k) is the gradient. \mathbf{r} is the ray vector in reference space. Termination and Validation: If the maximum number of iterations is reached and the fragment is not projected onto the isosurface within an error tolerance bound, or if the point has moved outside the cell, the fragment is discarded. If successfully projected, shading is computed and the corresponding pixel color is updated. Pitfall: Fragments are processed independently, making it impossible to detect and directly fix holes in the isosurface during this phase. Holes can result from insufficient iteration counts, high curvature relative to initial sampling, or insufficient quad sizes. Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def dot(a, b): return sum(ai bi for ai, bi in zip(a, b)) def magnitudesq(v): return dot(v, v) def generateuniformseedincell(cell): # Not given in the sources. Standard uniform distribution over cell bounds is assumed. return [cell.min[i] + (cell.max[i] - cell.min[i]) 0.5 for i in range(len(cell.min))] def integrategradientstep(x, gradf, stepsize): # Not given in the sources. Standard Euler integration is assumed. g = gradf(x) return [xi + stepsize gi for xi, gi in zip(x, g)] def isinsidecell(x, cell): # Not given in the sources. Standard bounds check is assumed. return all(cmin <= xi <= cmax for xi, (cmin, cmax) in zip(x, zip(cell.min, cell.max))) def isvalidprojection(x, cell, f, tolerance): # Not given in the sources. Standard bounds and value check is assumed. return abs(f(x)) < tolerance and isinsidecell(x, cell) def createsurfacetangentquad(center, normal): # Not given in the sources. Standard tangent plane quad generation is assumed. return {"center": center, "normal": normal} def rasterizequads(quads): # Not given in the sources. Standard GPU rasterization is assumed. return [{"position": q["center"], "raydirection": q["normal"]} for q in quads] def transformtoreference(coord): # Not given in the sources. Standard coordinate transformation is assumed. return list(coord) def computeshading(x, gradf): # Not given in the sources. Standard shading computation is assumed. return [0.5, 0.5, 0.5] def directionalnewtonraphsongradient(x, f, gradf, maxiter, tolerance): for k in range(maxiter): fx = f(x) if abs(fx) < tolerance: break g = gradf(x) gdotg = magnitudesq(g) if gdotg == 0: break step = (fx / gdotg) g x = [xi - s for xi, s in zip(x, step)] return x def directionalnewtonraphsonray(x, r, f, gradf, maxiter, tolerance): for k in range(maxiter): fx = f(x) if abs(fx) < tolerance: break g = gradf(x) gdotr = dot(g, r) if gdotr == 0: break step = (fx / gdotr) r x = [xi - s for xi, s in zip(x, step)] return x def objectspacesampling(f, gradf, domaincells, seedspercell, stepsize, maxiter, tolerance): quads = [] for cell in domaincells: for in range(seedspercell): x = generateuniformseedincell(cell) x = integrategradientstep(x, gradf, stepsize) x = directionalnewtonraphsongradient(x, f, gradf, maxiter, tolerance) if isvalidprojection(x, cell, f, tolerance): normal = gradf(x) quad = createsurfacetangentquad(center=x, normal=normal) quads.append(quad) return quads def imagespacerefinement(quads, cells, f, gradf, maxiter, tolerance): fragments = rasterizequads(quads) results = [] for frag, cell in zip(fragments, cells): x = transformtoreference(frag["position"]) r = transformtoreference(frag["raydirection"]) for k in range(maxiter): fx = f(x) if abs(fx) < tolerance: break g = gradf(x) gdotr = dot(g, r) if gdotr == 0: break step = (fx / gdotr) r x = [xi - s for xi, s in zip(x, step)] if not isinsidecell(x, cell): break if isvalidprojection(x, cell, f, tolerance): color = computeshading(x, gradf(x)) results.append((frag["pixelid"], color)) else: pass return results This is the second phase of the iHOS method. It takes the surface-tangent quads generated by the object space sampling and rasterizes them in image space. Fragments from this rasterization serve as starting points for ray casting. Directional Newton-Raphson is applied along the ray direction to refine the isosurface representation. It is chosen when high-quality, pixel-accurate rendering is required after the initial coarse sampling. A key limitation is that fragments are processed independently, making it impossible to detect and directly fix holes in the isosurface during this phase. Time Complexity: The method performs independent computations for each seed and fragment. The complexity is linear with respect to the number of seeds and fragments, multiplied by the cost of polynomial evaluation and gradient computation per iteration. Memory Complexity: Low, as no neighborhood information is needed. Data structures are limited to the seed points and the resulting quads. Strengths: The gradient integration step significantly improves the robustness of Newton-Raphson convergence by ensuring starting points are close to the isosurface. The use of directional NR provides quadratic convergence. Limitations: Holes in the isosurface can occur due to insufficient iteration counts, high curvature, or insufficient quad sizes. These holes cannot be fixed in the image-space phase because fragments are processed independently. When to use: Suitable for interactive rendering of high-order polynomial isosurfaces where robustness of root finding is critical. When not to use: Not suitable if hole-free surfaces are required without post-processing, or if the isosurface has extremely high curvature relative to the initial sampling density. The method was introduced by C. Pagot, J. Vollrath, F. Sadlo, D. Weiskopf, T. Ertl, and J.L.D. Comba in the paper "Interactive Isocontouring of High-Order Surfaces" (2011). It is part of a GPU-based approach for interactive rendering of isosurfaces defined by high-order multivariate polynomials. The method builds on the principle that standard Newton-Raphson methods require good initial guesses, which are provided by gradient integration in object space. Interactive Isocontouring of High-Order Surfaces, https://doi.org/10.4230/dfu.vol2.sciviz.2011.276, fetched 2026-10-29 (book)

Adaptive Ray Marching for GPIS Intersection is a geometric tracing algorithm that marches along a ray with adaptive step sizes to locate the first zero crossing of a Gaussian Process Implicit Surface (GPIS). It solves the problem of efficiently finding isosurface intersections in probabilistic implicit fields by drastically reducing the number of expensive GP samples compared to fixed discretization methods. Unlike fixed-step approaches, it dynamically adjusts the step size based on statistical bounds of the GP mean and covariance to ensure the probability of missing a root remains below a user-specified tolerance. Inputs: Ray origin \mathbf{x}0 and direction  oldsymbol{\omega} (normalized). GPIS model defined by mean function \mu(\mathbf{x}), covariance function k(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}), and their derivatives. Tolerance parameter \eta (default 0.001). Lipschitz constant of the mean \Lambda\mu. Uniform bound on covariance \sigma (where \sigma^2 = \sup{\mathbf{x}} |k(\mathbf{x})|). Uniform bound on the second derivative of the covariance M (where M = \sup{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}} \left|  rac{\partial^2 k}{\partial \mathbf{x} \partial \mathbf{y}}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) ight|). Constants C\eta and Q\eta derived from \eta and the ratio \sqrt{M}/\Lambda\mu. Minimum step size \Delta{\min}. Outputs: Intersection distance 	au along the ray. Intersection point \mathbf{x}	au = \mathbf{x}0 + 	au oldsymbol{\omega}. Surface normal \mathbf{n}s =  rac{
abla f(\mathbf{x}	au)}{\|
abla f(\mathbf{x}	au)\|}. Data Structures: A history of sampled points along the ray to condition the Online Gaussian Process Sampler. Each entry stores the parameter t and the sampled value v = f(\mathbf{x}0 + t oldsymbol{\omega}). Assumptions: The mean \mu and covariance k are continuously differentiable. \mu is Lipschitz-continuous with constant \Lambda\mu. k and its second derivative are uniformly bounded by \sigma^2 and M respectively. Algorithm Steps: 1. Initialize the ray parameter ti = 0 and sample the initial value vi = f(\mathbf{x}0 + ti oldsymbol{\omega}) using the Online Gaussian Process Sampler. Store (ti, vi) in the history. 2. Determine the adaptive step size \Deltai using the bounds derived from the GP statistics (see Formulas below). 3. Advance the ray parameter: t{i+1} = ti + \Deltai. 4. Sample the function value at the new position: v{i+1} = f(\mathbf{x}0 + t{i+1} oldsymbol{\omega}) using the Online Gaussian Process Sampler, conditioning on all previous samples including (ti, vi). Store (t{i+1}, v{i+1}) in the history. 5. Check for root crossing: If vi \cdot v{i+1} > 0, the interval [ti, t{i+1}] is considered to contain no root. Set ti = t{i+1}, vi = v{i+1}, and repeat from Step 2. If vi \cdot v{i+1} \leq 0, the interval is considered to contain exactly one root. Proceed to Step 6. 6. Locate the root 	au within [ti, t{i+1}] using a bracketed root solver (e.g., bisection or Dekker-Brent). During the root solving process, evaluate f at query points using the Online Gaussian Process Sampler, appending each new evaluation to the history to condition future evaluations. 7. Return 	au as the intersection distance. 8. Compute the surface normal \mathbf{n}s at \mathbf{x}	au using the gradient 
abla f sampled via the GP model. Formulas: The step size \Deltai is chosen such that the probability of missing a root in [ti, ti + \Deltai] is no more than \eta. Assuming f(ti) > 0 (if f(ti) < 0, the signs are inverted in the logic), the lower bound of f on the interval is given by: \inf{[ti, t{i+1}]} f \geq \mu(ti) - \Lambda\mu \Deltai - Q\eta \sigma \inf{[ti, t{i+1}]} f \geq f(ti) - (\Lambda\mu + C\eta \sqrt{M}) \Deltai with probability no less than 1 - \eta. The step size \Deltai is calculated by ensuring the lower bound remains positive (assuming f(ti) > 0) or by solving for the maximum \Deltai that satisfies the tolerance. The source provides the clamping logic: \Deltai = \max \left( \Delta{\min}, \dots ight) The exact numerator for the first bound is |\mu(ti)| - Q\eta \sigma. If this value is negative or zero, the step size is limited by the second bound or \Delta{\min}. The source text cuts off the full explicit formula for \Deltai, but implies it is derived from setting the lower bound inequalities to zero or a safe margin. Specifically, from the first inequality: \Deltai \leq  rac{\mu(ti) - Q\eta \sigma}{\Lambda\mu} From the second inequality: \Deltai \leq  rac{f(ti)}{\Lambda\mu + C\eta \sqrt{M}} The algorithm takes the minimum of these valid upper bounds, clamped by \Delta{\min}. Parameters: \eta: User-specified tolerance for the probability of missing a root. Typical value: 0.001. C\eta: Constant controlling tail probability \eta. For \eta = 0.001, C\eta \approx 4.5. Q\eta: Constant controlling tail probability \eta, depending on \sqrt{M}/\Lambda\mu. For \eta = 0.001, Q\eta \approx 4.1 when \sqrt{M}/\Lambda\mu = 1 and Q\eta \approx 5.4 when \sqrt{M}/\Lambda\mu = 1000. \Lambda\mu: Lipschitz constant of the mean function \mu, defined as \sup{\mathbf{x} 
eq \mathbf{y}} |\mu(\mathbf{x}) - \mu(\mathbf{y})| / \|\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y}\|. \sigma: Uniform bound on the covariance function k, defined as \sup{\mathbf{x}} |k(\mathbf{x})|. M: Uniform bound on the second derivative of the covariance function, defined as \sup{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}} \left|  rac{\partial^2 k}{\partial \mathbf{x} \partial \mathbf{y}}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) ight|. Pitfalls: If the interval [ti, t{i+1}] is chosen too large, multiple zero crossings may exist within it (cases c and d in Figure 3 of the source), causing errors in root finding. The adaptive step size limits this risk. The algorithm assumes the mean \mu and covariance k are continuously differentiable, \mu is Lipschitz-continuous, and k and its second derivative are uniformly bounded. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def adaptiveraymarchinggpis(x0, omega, gpmodel, eta, Lambdamu, sigma, M, Deltamin): """ Finds the first zero crossing of a GPIS along a ray. Args: x0: Ray origin (3D vector). omega: Ray direction (normalized 3D vector). gpmodel: Object providing mean, covariance, gradient, and online sampling. eta: Tolerance for missing root (default 0.001). Lambdamu: Lipschitz constant of mean. sigma: Uniform bound on covariance. M: Uniform bound on second derivative of covariance. Deltamin: Minimum step size. Returns: tau: Intersection distance. ns: Surface normal. """ # Precompute constants based on eta and ratio sqrt(M)/Lambdamu # Note: Exact mapping for Qeta and Ceta depends on supplemental document. # For eta=0.001: Ceta approx 4.5. Qeta approx 4.1 (if ratio=1) to 5.4 (if ratio=1000). Ceta = 4.5 ratio = sqrt(M) / Lambdamu if ratio <= 1: Qeta = 4.1 elif ratio >= 1000: Qeta = 5.4 else: # Interpolate or use exact formula from supplemental doc Qeta = 4.1 # Initialize ti = 0.0 # Sample initial point vi = gpmodel.sampleonline(x0 + ti omega) history = [(ti, vi)] while True: # Determine step size Deltai # Calculate upper bounds for Deltai to ensure probability of missing root < eta # Assuming f(ti) > 0 logic applies; if f(ti) < 0, use absolute values or invert logic valmu = gpmodel.mean(x0 + ti omega) valf = vi # f(ti) # Bound 1: based on mean # inf f >= mu(ti) - Lambdamu Delta - Qeta sigma # To ensure inf f > 0 (assuming mu > 0), Delta <= (mu - Qeta sigma) / Lambdamu bound1num = abs(valmu) - Qeta sigma if bound1num > 0: delta1 = bound1num / Lambdamu else: delta1 = float('inf') # This bound doesn't restrict if mean is already small/negative # Bound 2: based on sampled value # inf f >= f(ti) - (Lambdamu + Ceta sqrt(M)) Delta # To ensure inf f > 0 (assuming f > 0), Delta <= f(ti) / (Lambdamu + Ceta sqrt(M)) denom2 = Lambdamu + Ceta sqrt(M) if denom2 > 0: delta2 = abs(valf) / denom2 else: delta2 = float('inf') # Choose step size Deltai = min(delta1, delta2) Deltai = max(Deltai, Deltamin) # Advance tnext = ti + Deltai vnext = gpmodel.sampleonline(x0 + tnext omega, history) history.append((tnext, vnext)) # Check for root crossing if vi vnext > 0: # No root in interval ti = tnext vi = vnext else: # Root bracketed [ti, tnext] # Use bracketed root solver (e.g., Brent's method) tau = solverootbrent(lambda t: gpmodel.sampleonline(x0 + t omega, history), ti, tnext) # Compute normal at intersection gradf = gpmodel.gradient(x0 + tau omega) ns = gradf / norm(gradf) return tau, ns A sibling method that uses a hierarchical grid to find volume maxima. It is chosen when rendering volumetric data rather than implicit surfaces, and does not rely on GP statistical bounds for step sizing. Time Complexity: The method reduces the number of GP samples by a cubic factor compared to fixed discretization. The number of steps depends on the smoothness of the GP; smoother surfaces allow larger steps. Memory Complexity: Requires storing the history of samples for the Online Gaussian Process Sampler. The size of the history grows with the number of steps, but is generally much smaller than the full GP kernel matrix required for batch sampling. Strengths: Drastically reduces computation by taking large, conservative steps. Provides probabilistic guarantees on not missing roots. Limitations: Requires precomputed or analytically computable bounds (\Lambda\mu, \sigma, M). Relies on the Online GP Sampler, which has its own computational cost per sample. When to use: Rendering GPIS where the GP model is available and bounds are known. When not to use: When the GP bounds are unknown or difficult to compute, or when the GP is not continuously differentiable. The method was introduced in "Adaptive Ray Marching for Rendering Gaussian Process Implicit Surfaces". It addresses the high computational cost of rendering GPIS by replacing fixed-step ray marching with an adaptive approach that leverages the statistical properties of the Gaussian Process to determine safe step sizes. The approach builds on the Online Gaussian Process Sampler to allow efficient incremental sampling along the ray. Adaptive Ray Marching for Rendering Gaussian Process Implicit Surfaces, https://doi.org/10.1145/3799902.3811101, fetched 2026-10-28

Fast Shading for Voxel Engines is a CPU-based lighting algorithm for voxel engines that assumes vertical sun rays. It modifies voxel palette indices to simulate lighting by checking immediate neighbors, operating on palettes where indices 1–32, 33–64, etc., represent dark-to-light gradients. It is faster but less visually detailed than its sibling fastshade1, using fewer neighbor checks. Compared to Voxel Shading Pipeline, Voxel Shader Volumetric Ray Marching, and Voxmat Path Tracer, it avoids ray casting and floating-point math, trading accuracy for CPU-only speed. Inputs: A 3D voxel grid of dimensions xsize 	imes ysize 	imes zsize, where each voxel stores a palette index. Outputs: The same voxel grid with modified palette indices representing shaded lighting. Data Structures: The voxel grid is indexed by integer coordinates (x, y, z). Each voxel contains a single integer representing a palette index. The palette is structured such that indices 1 to 32 represent the first tone group, 33 to 64 the second, and so on. Parameters: xsize, ysize, zsize: The dimensions of the current level in voxels. Algorithm Steps: 1. Iterate x from 1 to xsize-2, y from 1 to ysize-2, and z from 1 to zsize-2. 2. Retrieve the current voxel's palette index: v = 	ext{get\voxel\pal}(x, y, z). 3. If v = 0 (empty), skip to the next voxel. 4. Initialize a shading offset d = 0. 5. Check neighbors to determine occlusion/shading based on the assumption of vertical sun rays: If the voxel at (x, y+1, z) is occupied (v{top} 
eq 0), subtract 3 from the potential brightness (add 3 to offset d). If any of the voxels at (x+1, y+1, z), (x-1, y+1, z), (x+1, y+1, z+1), or (x+1, y+1, z-1) are occupied, subtract 2 from the potential brightness (add 2 to offset d). If any of the voxels at (x+1, y, z+1), (x-1, y, z-1), (x+1, y, z-1), or (x-1, y, z+1) are occupied, subtract 1 from the potential brightness (add 1 to offset d). 6. Calculate the new index: v{new} = v - d. 7. Clamp the new index to the lower bound of its tone group: if v{new} < m, then v{new} = m. 8. Write the modified voxel index back: 	ext{set\voxel\pal}(x, y, z, v{new}). Formulas: Tone group lower bound (color margin): m = \lfloor (v-1)/32 floor 	imes 32 + 1, where v is the current palette index and \lfloor \cdot floor denotes the floor function. Shading offset: d = 3 \cdot \mathbb{I}(v{x,y+1,z} 
eq 0) + 2 \cdot \mathbb{I}(	ext{any of } v{x\pm1,y+1,z}, v{x+1,y+1,z\pm1} 
eq 0) + 1 \cdot \mathbb{I}(	ext{any of } v{x\pm1,y,z\pm1} 
eq 0), where \mathbb{I}(\cdot) is the indicator function. Edge Cases and Pitfalls: The voxel index must not drop below the lower bound of its tone group (m). If v{new} < m, clamp it to m. Boundary voxels are excluded from processing to avoid out-of-bounds memory access. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def fastshadevoxels(grid, xsize, ysize, zsize): for x in range(1, xsize - 1): for y in range(1, ysize - 1): for z in range(1, zsize - 1): v = grid[x, y, z] # Skip empty voxels if v == 0: continue # Calculate tone group lower bound colormargin = ((v - 1) // 32) 32 + 1 shadingoffset = 0 # Weight 3: Directly above if grid[x, y + 1, z] != 0: shadingoffset += 3 # Weight 2: Upper neighbors (specific coordinates per source) if (grid[x + 1, y + 1, z] != 0 or grid[x - 1, y + 1, z] != 0 or grid[x + 1, y + 1, z + 1] != 0 or grid[x + 1, y + 1, z - 1] != 0): shadingoffset += 2 # Weight 1: Side neighbors (specific coordinates per source) if (grid[x + 1, y, z + 1] != 0 or grid[x - 1, y, z - 1] != 0 or grid[x + 1, y, z - 1] != 0 or grid[x - 1, y, z + 1] != 0): shadingoffset += 1 # Apply shading newv = v - shadingoffset # Clamp to tone group lower bound if newv < colormargin: newv = colormargin grid[x, y, z] = newv fastshade1 is a fast shading algorithm for voxel engines that assumes vertical sun rays. It modifies voxel palette indices to simulate lighting, working with palettes where indices 1-32, 33-64, etc., represent dark-to-light gradients. It is faster but less visually detailed than its sibling fastshade2, using fewer neighbor checks. Time Complexity: O(N) where N is the number of voxels, as it iterates through the grid once. The constant factor is low due to simple integer arithmetic and neighbor lookups. Memory Complexity: O(1) auxiliary space, operating in-place on the voxel grid. Performance: Designed for CPU-only rendering engines. It is significantly faster than ray-marching or complex normal-based shading because it avoids floating-point lighting calculations and long-distance ray casts. Strengths: Extremely fast, suitable for real-time CPU rendering in voxel engines. Simple to implement. Limitations: Visual quality is limited by the discrete palette steps and the simplified lighting model (vertical sun rays only). It does not support dynamic light sources or complex material properties. The shading is static relative to the voxel structure. Fast Shading for Voxel Engines was developed as part of the DOOMMY voxel engine, a voxel-based game engine that uses only CPU for rendering and Lua for scripting. The engine was created by jabberx in 2012. The method is designed to provide basic lighting effects in a resource-constrained environment without GPU acceleration. jabberx/doommy, https://github.com/jabberx/doommy, fetched 2026-10-27 (unofficial)

MIP-Splatting is a rendering method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that eliminates aliasing and dilation artifacts when rendering at resolutions, focal lengths, or camera distances different from those encountered during training. It solves the mismatch between the discrete sampling of the image sensor and the continuous representation of 3D Gaussians by introducing a multi-scale representation and two specific filters: a 3D smoothing filter and a 2D Mip filter. Unlike standard 3DGS which suffers from high-frequency artifacts when zooming out and dilation effects when zooming in, MIP-Splatting constrains the representation frequency to the Nyquist limit of the training data and approximates the physical box filter of a camera sensor. Inputs: A set of 3D Gaussians \{Gk\} parameterized by center pk, covariance \Sigmak, opacity \alphak, and spherical harmonics coefficients; a camera defined by projection matrix and intrinsics; and rendering resolution. Outputs: A rendered image and visibility radii. Data Structures: Each Gaussian Gk stores: pk \in \mathbb{R}^3: Center position. \Sigmak \in \mathbb{R}^{3 	imes 3}: Covariance matrix, parameterized as \Sigmak = Ok sk sk^T Ok^T, where s \in \mathbb{R}^3 is a scaling vector and O \in \mathbb{R}^{3 	imes 3} is a rotation matrix from a quaternion. \alphak \in [0, 1]: Opacity. SHk: Spherical harmonics features. lk \in \{1, \dots, L{max}\}: Level of detail index. S{k}^{max}, S{k}^{min} \in \mathbb{R}: Maximum and minimum pixel coverage thresholds for selective rendering. Steps: 1. 3D Smoothing Filter (Training): To prevent the optimization from creating Gaussians with frequencies higher than the training data can support, a 3D low-pass filter is applied. The maximal sampling frequency \hat{
u} is derived from the training views. For a camera at depth d with focal length f, the world space sampling interval is \hat{T} = d/f, so \hat{
u} = f/d. The regularized 3D Gaussian Gk(x){reg} is the convolution of Gk with a low-pass Gaussian G{low} with variance \hat{
u}k^s \cdot I, where s is a scalar hyperparameter (typical value 0.2): Gk(x){reg} =  rac{\sqrt{|\Sigmak|}}{\sqrt{|\Sigmak + \hat{
u}k^s \cdot I|}} e^{- rac{1}{2}(x-pk)^T (\Sigmak + \hat{
u}k^s \cdot I)^{-1} (x-pk)} This effectively increases the 3D covariance: \Sigmak' = \Sigmak + \hat{
u}k^s \cdot I. The maximal sampling rate is recomputed every m iterations (typical m=100). 2. Multi-Scale Aggregation (Training): The scene is represented by Gaussians at L{max}=4 levels (1x, 4x, 16x, 64x downsampled resolution). Initialization: All Gaussians start at level l=1. Aggregation Trigger: After a warm-up stage (first 1,000 iterations), coarse-level Gaussians are created. Selection: For each coarse level lm (2 \le lm \le L{max}), render all Gaussians from levels [1, lm-1] at 4^{lm-1} times downsampled resolution. Gaussians with pixel coverage Sk < ST (threshold ST=2 pixels) are selected as "too small". Voxelization: Selected Gaussians are binned into a voxel grid of resolution (400/lm)^3. Pooling: Within each voxel, attributes (position, rotation, SH, opacity, scaling) are averaged. Enlargement: The new Gaussian's scaling is enlarged by factor ST / S{avg}, where S{avg} is the average pixel coverage of the aggregated Gaussians, targeting a pixel coverage of ST. The new Gaussian is assigned to level lm. Training: The model is trained on both original and downsampled images. 3. Selective Rendering (Inference): At render time, Gaussians are selected based on their current pixel coverage Sk relative to their stored bounds Sk^{max} and Sk^{min}. Update Bounds: If the rendering resolution scale matches the scale at which Gk was created, update bounds: Sk^{max'} = \max(\lambda1 Sk^{max}, Sk), \quad Sk^{min'} = \min(\lambda2 Sk^{min}, Sk) where \lambda1 = 0.95 and \lambda2 = 1.05. Selection Condition: A Gaussian is rendered if:  rac{Sk}{Sk^{max}} \le S{rel}^{max} \quad \land \quad \left(  rac{Sk}{Sk^{min}} \ge S{rel}^{min} \lor Sk \ge ST ight) where S{rel}^{max} = 1.5 and S{rel}^{min} = 0.5. This filters out Gaussians that are too large (causing blur) or too small (causing aliasing), except those larger than ST which are safe. 4. 2D Mip Filter (Rendering): To handle dilation artifacts when zooming in (where projected Gaussians become small but the fixed dilation k