Principled Uncertainty Pruning (PUP) is a post-hoc method for compressing 3D Gaussian Splatting (3D-GS) models by removing Gaussians with high uncertainty regarding their contribution to the reconstruction error. Unlike heuristic methods that rely on opacity or visibility, PUP computes a sensitivity score based on the Fisher Information matrix of the L2 reconstruction loss, specifically approximating the Hessian with respect to spatial parameters. It is distinguished by its multi-round prune-refine pipeline, which allows for aggressive pruning (up to 90%) while maintaining higher visual fidelity and rendering speeds than single-round approaches or competing methods like LightGaussian. Inputs and Outputs: Input: A converged 3D-GS model \mathcal{G} = \{g1, \dots, gN\}, the set of training views \mathcal{P}{gt} with ground truth images I{gt}, and camera poses \Phi. Output: A pruned set of Gaussians \mathcal{G}' and fine-tuned parameters. Data Structures: Each Gaussian gi retains its standard 3D-GS parameters: position \mui \in \mathbb{R}^3, scale si \in \mathbb{R}^3 (or covariance \Sigmai), rotation qi \in \mathbb{R}^4, opacity \alphai \in \mathbb{R}, and spherical harmonic coefficients for color. PUP computes a scalar pruning score ui for each Gaussian. Method Steps: 1. Compute the L2 Reconstruction Loss: Calculate the total squared error over all training views: L2 =  rac{1}{2} \sum{\phi \in \mathcal{P}{gt}} \| I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) - I{gt} \|2^2 where I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) is the rendered image for pose \phi. 2. Approximate the Hessian via Fisher Information: The full Hessian of the loss with respect to Gaussian parameters \mathcal{G} is: 
abla{\mathcal{G}}^2 L2 = \sum{\phi \in \mathcal{P}{gt}} 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi)^T + (I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) - I{gt}) 
abla{\mathcal{G}}^2 I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) On a converged model, the residual term (I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) - I{gt}) approaches zero, causing the second-order term to vanish. The Hessian is approximated by the Fisher Information matrix: 
abla{\mathcal{G}}^2 L2 \approx \sum{\phi \in \mathcal{P}{gt}} 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) 
abla{\mathcal{G}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi)^T 3. Compute Block-Wise Sensitivity Score: To manage memory and focus on geometric uncertainty, the Fisher matrix is approximated block-wise for each Gaussian gi. The score is computed exclusively for the mean (\mui) and scale (si) parameters, resulting in a 6 	imes 6 matrix per Gaussian. Rotations and RGB color parameters are excluded. The pruning score ui for Gaussian i is the log-determinant of this block: ui = \log \det \left( \sum{\phi \in \mathcal{P}{gt}} 
abla{\{\mui, si\}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi) 
abla{\{\mui, si\}} I{\mathcal{G}}(\phi)^T ight) This score represents the entropy of the second-order Laplace approximation of the posterior distribution for that Gaussian; higher entropy indicates higher uncertainty and lower sensitivity to parameter changes, making the Gaussian a candidate for removal. 4. Patch-Based Computation: The sensitivity score is computed over image patches rather than full images to reduce computational cost. The default patch size is 4 	imes 4 pixels, though 2 	imes 2 and 8 	imes 8 are also viable. 5. Multi-Round Prune-Refine Pipeline: The method applies pruning and fine-tuning iteratively: Round 1: Prune the top 80% of Gaussians with the highest scores ui. Fine-tune the remaining 20% for 5,000 iterations. Round 2: Prune the top 50% of the remaining Gaussians. Fine-tune for another 5,000 iterations. This two-round process achieves approximately 90% total pruning. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def principleduncertaintypruning(model, trainingviews, numrounds=2, round1prunepct=0.8, round2prunepct=0.5, finetuneiters=5000, patchsize=(4, 4)): """ model: 3D-GS model with parameters G, converged. trainingviews: List of (pose, gtimage) tuples. """ gaussians = model.gaussians ngaussians = len(gaussians) # Step 1 & 2 & 3: Compute Fisher Information based pruning scores # We compute the 6x6 Fisher block for mean and scale for each Gaussian # Memory usage: N 36 floats (for 6x6 matrix) fisherblocks = computefisherblocks(gaussians, trainingviews, patchsize) # Step 3 continued: Compute pruning score as log determinant scores = [] for i in range(ngaussians): Fi = fisherblocks[i] # 6x6 matrix # Guard against singular matrices if issingular(Fi): scores.append(0.0) else: scores.append(logdet(Fi)) scores = array(scores) # Step 5: Multi-round Prune-Refine currentgaussians = gaussians currentscores = scores for roundidx in range(numrounds): 